<p class="muted">Teacher planning report • Reviewed October 10, 2026 • Recommendations only</p>
<h1>Unit 5: Targeted improvements</h1>
<p class="lead">The strongest improvement is a clearer bridge from definitions and simulations to independent problem solving. Sound, lenses, and spectral shifts need that bridge most urgently.</p>
<p>Unit 5 already contains substantial interactive material. Keep the useful simulations and the existing oscillator worked example. Replace repeated vocabulary blocks with measured diagrams, complete numerical models, and short challenges that require students to explain their evidence.</p>
<p class="muted">Scope: reviewed the learner-preview text and structure of all 15 sections across the five live Rise courses; compared them with seven local application packages and selected current HTML activities. This was a curriculum and selected-source-code audit, not an exhaustive test of every accordion, answer key, device size, video, or embedded interaction. Local files are not proof that their latest versions are deployed. The honors recommendations concern local extension materials. No course content, grades, or application code was changed.</p>

<h2>1. What to do first</h2>
<table><tr><th>Priority</th><th>Change</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Add real/virtual thin-lens walkthroughs to U5L3 and harmonic/resonance walkthroughs to U5L2.</td><td>The applications require calculations and interpretation beyond the worked instruction visible in Rise.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Correct wave-speed, photon-energy, color, wireless-frequency, and solar-fusion statements.</td><td>These can create misconceptions that later activities reinforce.</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Make spectral-shift calculations a required preparation step before the U5L5 application.</td><td>The current conceptual explanation does not model the application's numerical method.</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>Reuse the existing photoelectric, nuclear-decay, and half-life activities after the targeted corrections below.</td><td>These already provide stronger evidence gathering than another text-only process block.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Replace repeated recall checks with prediction, calculation, and explanation pairs.</td><td>Students should demonstrate transfer, not merely recognize a term or the longest answer.</td></tr></table>

<h2>2. Lesson-by-lesson changes</h2>
<h3>U5L1 • Wave properties</h3>
<p><b>Keep:</b> particle-versus-wave motion, the pulse and spring models, boundary/interference simulations, and the existing example using 40 vibrations in 30 s and a crest traveling 4.25 m in 10 s. That example genuinely models a multi-step solution.</p>
<p><b>Replace/add:</b> replace one of the two vocabulary-matching activities with paired graphs: displacement against position and displacement against time. Have students mark amplitude, wavelength, and period, then calculate frequency and speed. Include a peak-to-peak trap and milliseconds-to-seconds conversion. Label the existing oscillator question as a prediction/pretest, or place a different-number worked example before it.</p>
<p>Before the boundary simulator, show one three-column comparison: same source, slower wave speed, shorter wavelength, unchanged frequency. Follow interference with a signed-displacement calculation and an animation showing both pulses continuing afterward.</p>
<p><b>Clean up:</b> the matching directions refer to dropdowns although the reviewed interaction uses pairing controls. Replace answer-revealing names such as <code>Transverse_Wave_Definition</code> with meaningful image descriptions. Correct “how the changing the amplitude.” Replace the ambulance-siren introduction to boundaries with an echo, doorway diffraction, or a refracted ray.</p>

<h3>U5L2 • Sound</h3>
<p><b>Keep:</b> Anatomy of Sound, Speed of Sound Explorer, Timbre &amp; Tuning Studio, and the standing-wave visualization.</p>
<p><b>Replace/add:</b> put a labeled displacement-mode diagram directly before the instrument and resonance applications. Work from the picture to the fraction of a wavelength in the tube, then to the equation. Show one open tube and one tube closed at one end, including why the latter has only odd harmonics in the ideal model. Model one resonance-table row, unit conversion, average speed, and deviation from the reference speed before students complete the full investigation.</p>
<p>Add a quiet, user-activated beat-matching challenge: students hear or see beats, adjust a frequency, and determine whether their first adjustment helped. Do not autoplay loud sounds. Keep numerical moving-source Doppler formulas optional unless they are assessed; strengthen the conceptual Doppler activity with line-of-sight arrows and compressed/expanded wavefronts.</p>
<p><b>Application alignment:</b> the Perfect Instrument task asks for lengths, harmonics, temperature effects, and an impossible even-harmonic case. The Rise preparation should model those reasoning types with different numbers. Its open-versus-stopped comparison should hold target frequency constant when testing the half-length relationship.</p>

<h3>U5L3 • Light, optics, and vision</h3>
<p><b>Keep:</b> the EM spectrum overview, frequency/wavelength/photon-energy interactive, and the real-image screen test.</p>
<p><b>Replace/add:</b> reduce the repeated spectrum/color lists. Add an actual numerical wavelength conversion in the first section. In the lens section, replace the generic “rearrange and substitute” style of instruction with the complete paired examples in this report. Each should show a ray diagram, signs, equation, substitution, answer, and physical check. Cover the object inside the focal length and the limiting case at the focal point.</p>
<p>The local thin-lens application has substantial signed numerical work. Students need a compact sign-convention card and practice finding image distance, magnification, image height, and focal length before it. Introduce Snell's law calculations only where they support the intended refraction task; do not add a new assessed requirement merely because an interactive exists.</p>
<p>For color, use three linked panels showing the source spectrum, what the filter/object transmits or reflects, and the cone responses. Let students make yellow from red and green light, then compare it with single-wavelength yellow. Their task is to explain similar appearance without claiming the spectra are identical.</p>

<h3>U5L4 • Wireless communication and astronomy</h3>
<p><b>Keep:</b> the familiar Bluetooth/Wi-Fi/cellular/satellite contexts and multiwavelength astronomy connection.</p>
<p><b>Replace/add:</b> replace one repeated EM-spectrum catalog with a four-bit message demonstration: choose a bit pattern, modulate a carrier, add noise, and recover the message. Separate carrier frequency, bandwidth, signal strength, and data rate. Include a carrier-wavelength calculation and a propagation-delay estimate.</p>
<p>The local <code>connection_protocol_lab.html</code> already has four technology-selection scenarios. Extend it with competing constraints such as range, energy use, obstruction, and delay. Require students to justify a choice from the displayed data instead of selecting the obvious device pairing. Label the numbers as scenario assumptions rather than universal technology limits.</p>
<p>Replace the “everyday versus science/medicine” spectrum sorting: those uses overlap. Ask which detector or wavelength band would reveal a specific feature and why. A same-object visible/infrared/X-ray comparison is stronger than another seven-region flashcard set.</p>

<h3>U5L5 • Modern physics and models</h3>
<p><b>Keep:</b> the threshold-frequency idea, individual-versus-population decay distinction, and emphasis on using evidence to select models.</p>
<p><b>Replace/add:</b> several process blocks describe a “simulation” without presenting an experiment students can manipulate in that block. Pair or replace them with the existing <code>photoelectric-effect.html</code>, <code>nuclear-decay-tracker.html</code>, and <code>half-life-population-lab.html</code>. Verify that the latest versions are actually embedded.</p>
<p>After the photon experiment, require a claim supported by two contrasting trials. After nuclear decay, work one alpha and one beta-minus equation by conserving mass number and charge. After the half-life model, calculate both a remaining amount and an elapsed time. In the final section, insert a numerical spectral-shift example before the application; it should not be hidden in optional enrichment.</p>
<p>End with an evidence case: a source gives individual detector hits that gradually form interference fringes. Students choose what each observation supports and identify the limitation of a classical wave-only or classical particle-only account. Avoid implying that light physically switches between two identities.</p>

<h2>3. Definitions and statements to repair</h2>
<table><tr><th>Location / current issue</th><th>Suggested teaching language or action</th></tr>
<tr><td>U5L1 media: lower density is linked generally to faster waves, then metal versus air is used as the example.</td><td>“Wave speed depends on the restoring forces and inertia of the medium. For a stretched string, greater tension raises speed and greater mass per length lowers it. Sound often travels faster in solids because their stiffness can outweigh their greater density.” Do not apply one density rule to every wave.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L1 diffraction: “greatest” spreading options include both comparable and much-smaller openings.</td><td>Use a clear comparison of an opening much wider than the wavelength with one comparable to it. Do not make “about equal” uniquely correct when a much-smaller opening is also offered without more constraints.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L2 sound speed explained only by particle closeness.</td><td>Include elasticity/stiffness and density, with actual material examples. Replace the ear-on-train-track example with sound through a tabletop.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L2 dB described as loudness; danger framed around 120 dB.</td><td>“Sound level is a logarithmic measure reported in decibels. Loudness is a perception affected by level and frequency.” Duration matters for hearing risk, including at lower levels; see <a href="https://www.nidcd.nih.gov/health/noise-induced-hearing-loss">NIDCD</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L2 open/closed tube nodes lack a quantity label.</td><td>Specify <b>air-displacement</b> nodes/antinodes. Pressure nodes and antinodes occur at the opposite locations. Resonance means a strong response when driving frequency is near a natural frequency; damping limits it.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L2 oscilloscope described as showing frequencies as peaks.</td><td>An ordinary oscilloscope trace shows signal versus time. A spectrum/FFT display shows amplitude versus frequency.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L3 frequency determines “how much energy the wave carries.”</td><td>“A photon's energy is E = hf. Total beam energy also depends on how many photons arrive.” Use same-color dim/bright beams and different-color equal-photon-count beams.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L3 converging-lens rays always meet on the other side.</td><td>State the condition d<sub>o</sub> &gt; f. For d<sub>o</sub> &lt; f, backward extensions locate an upright virtual image on the object side. Define focal length using incident parallel rays.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L3 perceived color assigned a single wavelength; screens create “every color.”</td><td>“Color perception depends on the light spectrum and the overlapping responses of three cone types. Different spectra can appear the same color. A display produces a limited range of colors.”</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L4 higher carrier frequency directly means faster data or shorter range.</td><td>Distinguish carrier frequency from bandwidth and data rate. Range also depends on power, antennas, obstacles, and receiver sensitivity. Higher-frequency systems may have wider available bandwidth; their waves do not thereby travel faster.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L4 Wi-Fi equated with internet; Bluetooth assigned a universal short range; GPS described as two-way.</td><td>Wi-Fi is local networking that can work without internet. Bluetooth range varies by implementation (<a href="https://www.bluetooth.com/learn-about-bluetooth/key-attributes/range/">Bluetooth SIG</a>). Ordinary GPS receivers receive satellite broadcasts and calculate position (<a href="https://www.gps.gov/systems/gps/index.php">GPS.gov</a>).</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L4 X-rays pass soft tissue but not bone.</td><td>Both attenuate X-rays by different amounts. Also distinguish infrared radiation from heat transfer, and say X-rays near black holes can come from surrounding hot matter.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L5 red light can never eject electrons; changing frequency cannot change photon number.</td><td>Name the material and its work function. A threshold depends on the material. Specify what is held constant: at fixed power, increasing photon energy decreases the number arriving per second.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L5 Sun process moves directly from two protons to helium.</td><td>Show a labeled simplified chain: its net result converts four hydrogen nuclei into helium-4, with other particles and energy released. Alternatively, show deuterium + tritium as a separate laboratory fusion example. See <a href="https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/themes/how-the-sun-shines/">How the Sun shines</a>.</td></tr>
<tr><td>U5L5 no spectral shift implies “staying still.”</td><td>In the simple Doppler model, no shift means no detected <b>radial</b> motion. Motion across the line of sight can still exist. Label v ≈ cz a low-speed approximation.</td></tr></table>

<h2>4. Worked examples to add, organized by problem type</h2>
<p>These are original tutorial examples, intended to use different numbers from assessed application questions. Reveal one decision at a time, then give a partially completed problem and one independent transfer problem. Use <b>read the diagram → list knowns → choose and rearrange → substitute with units → interpret/check</b>.</p>

<div class="example"><h3>1 • Read two wave graphs, then calculate</h3><p>A position graph has crests at 0.20 m and 0.80 m; a time graph has consecutive crests 150 ms apart. Maximum displacement is 3 cm.</p><p><b>Model:</b> λ = 0.80 − 0.20 = 0.60 m. T = 150 ms = 0.150 s. f = 1/T = 6.67 Hz. v = fλ = 4.00 m/s. A = 3 cm, while peak-to-peak displacement is 6 cm.</p><p><b>Transfer:</b> double frequency while holding the medium and tension constant. Students predict half the wavelength and unchanged speed, then test. Place in U5L1 section 2.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>2 • Add wave displacements</h3><p>At one point, one pulse gives +3.0 cm and another gives −2.0 cm. Net displacement is +1.0 cm. If they are +3.0 cm and −3.0 cm, the point is momentarily at equilibrium; the pulses continue afterward.</p><p><b>Transfer:</b> choose the overlap drawing and explain why zero displacement does not mean energy was destroyed. Place before the interference model.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>3 • Sound intensity with distance</h3><p>An approximately point-like source gives I = 8.0 × 10<sup>−4</sup> W/m² at 2.0 m. Assume spreading in open space with negligible absorption/reflection.</p><p>I<sub>2</sub> = I<sub>1</sub>(r<sub>1</sub>/r<sub>2</sub>)² = (8.0 × 10<sup>−4</sup>)(2.0/6.0)² = 8.9 × 10<sup>−5</sup> W/m². Tripling distance gives one-ninth the intensity, not one-third. Do not claim perceived loudness is one-ninth.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>4 • Open and stopped pipes at the same pitch</h3><p>Use v = 340 m/s and a target fundamental of 170 Hz. Ignore end correction.</p><p>Open at both ends: L = v/(2f) = 1.00 m. Closed at one end: L = v/(4f) = 0.500 m. Draw the displacement pattern that explains each denominator.</p><p>The open pipe supports 170, 340, 510… Hz. The stopped pipe supports 170, 510, 850… Hz. Its next mode is the third harmonic, not the second. <b>Transfer:</b> explain why 340 Hz cannot be the second allowed mode of that stopped pipe.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>5 • Resonance measurement and analysis</h3><p>A stopped tube resonates in its fundamental at 500 Hz when L = 17.0 cm. L = 0.170 m, λ = 4L = 0.680 m, so v = fλ = 340 m/s.</p><p>If three speed estimates are 338, 340, and 342 m/s, their mean is 340 m/s. Relative to 343 m/s, percentage deviation is |340 − 343|/343 × 100 = 0.875%, or about 0.9%. Call this percent error/deviation from a reference, rather than symmetric percent difference.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>6 • Light wavelength, frequency, and photon energy</h3><p>For 500 nm light in vacuum: λ = 5.00 × 10<sup>−7</sup> m. f = c/λ = 6.00 × 10<sup>14</sup> Hz. If photon calculations are required, E = hf = 3.98 × 10<sup>−19</sup> J ≈ 2.48 eV.</p><p><b>Check:</b> doubling the number of these photons doubles total energy but leaves energy per photon unchanged. Teach the photon-energy calculation in U5L5 if U5L3 is intended to remain conceptual.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>7 • Real image through a converging lens</h3><p>Given f = +20 cm, d<sub>o</sub> = +30 cm, h<sub>o</sub> = 3.0 cm. Use 1/f = 1/d<sub>o</sub> + 1/d<sub>i</sub>.</p><p>1/d<sub>i</sub> = 1/20 − 1/30 = 1/60, so d<sub>i</sub> = +60 cm. m = −d<sub>i</sub>/d<sub>o</sub> = −2.0. h<sub>i</sub> = mh<sub>o</sub> = −6.0 cm.</p><p><b>Meaning:</b> real, inverted, twice as tall, on the other side; it can be focused on a screen. Show principal rays meeting there. The equation and diagram should agree.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>8 • Move the same object inside the focal length</h3><p>Keep f = +20 cm, but d<sub>o</sub> = +10 cm. 1/d<sub>i</sub> = 1/20 − 1/10 = −1/20, so d<sub>i</sub> = −20 cm. m = −(−20)/10 = +2.0.</p><p><b>Meaning:</b> virtual, upright, twice as tall, on the object side. Actual transmitted rays diverge; dashed backward extensions meet. A screen cannot capture this virtual image. At d<sub>o</sub> = f, emerging paraxial rays are parallel and no finite image distance is obtained.</p><p><b>Transfer:</b> students diagnose a solution that reports a negative image distance as a calculation mistake.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>9 • Refraction with an angle measured from the normal</h3><p>If assessed: light enters glass (n = 1.50) from air (n ≈ 1.00) at 45.0° to the normal.</p><p>n<sub>1</sub>sinθ<sub>1</sub> = n<sub>2</sub>sinθ<sub>2</sub>; θ<sub>2</sub> = sin<sup>−1</sup>[(1.00/1.50)sin45.0°] = 28.1°. It bends toward the normal. The frequency stays the same while speed and wavelength decrease.</p><p>Optional extension for glass to air: θ<sub>c</sub> = sin<sup>−1</sup>(1.00/1.50) = 41.8°. Total internal reflection requires the higher-to-lower-index direction and an angle above this threshold.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>10 • Wireless wavelength and propagation delay</h3><p>A 2.4 GHz carrier has f = 2.4 × 10<sup>9</sup> Hz and λ ≈ (3.0 × 10<sup>8</sup>)/(2.4 × 10<sup>9</sup>) = 0.125 m = 12.5 cm.</p><p>For a separately specified satellite link with two 35,800 km legs, the one-way ground-to-satellite-to-ground path is 71,600 km. Time ≈ 71,600/300,000 = 0.239 s. A return message along the same path takes a total of about 0.477 s, before processing delays.</p><p><b>Check:</b> a higher carrier frequency alone does not shorten this travel time. This is a simplified geostationary-style path example, not a claim about all satellites or GPS.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>11 • Photoelectric threshold and maximum kinetic energy</h3><p>For a hypothetical surface with work function φ = 2.00 eV, a 500 nm photon has E ≈ 1240/500 = 2.48 eV. Since E &gt; φ, emission is possible and K<sub>max</sub> = E − φ = 0.48 eV.</p><p>At 700 nm, E ≈ 1.77 eV &lt; φ, so no emission occurs in this introductory single-photon model. More intensity at 700 nm does not overcome the threshold. Above threshold, greater photon flux can eject more electrons without increasing K<sub>max</sub>.</p><p>Use a clearly named different material or hypothetical surface for this tutorial; the local photoelectric activity uses its own work function.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>12 • Spectral shift and radial speed</h3><p>A known rest line at 500 nm is observed at 505 nm. z = (λ<sub>observed</sub> − λ<sub>rest</sub>)/λ<sub>rest</sub> = 5/500 = +0.010.</p><p>For small speeds relative to c, v<sub>r</sub> ≈ cz = 3,000 km/s, away from the observer. A second rest line at 600 nm should appear near 606 nm, showing the same fractional shift rather than the same 5 nm addition.</p><p><b>Transfer:</b> a zero shift rules out a detected radial speed in this model, not all motion. At larger shifts, explicitly identify the approximation's limits.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>13 • Half-life forward and backward</h3><p>A radioactive isotope has a 6-hour half-life. Of an initial 80 mg of that isotope, how much remains after 18 hours?</p><p>Number of half-lives = 18/6 = 3. Remaining isotope = 80(1/2)³ = 10 mg. Reverse problem: reaching 10 mg requires three halvings, hence 18 hours.</p><p><b>Meaning:</b> parent nuclei transform; the whole sample does not vanish. For a small number of nuclei, calculated remaining counts are statistical expectations, not guaranteed exact outcomes.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>14 • Nuclear bookkeeping and energy</h3><p>Alpha decay: <sup>238</sup><sub>92</sub>U → <sup>234</sup><sub>90</sub>Th + <sup>4</sup><sub>2</sub>He. Check 238 = 234 + 4 and 92 = 90 + 2.</p><p>Beta-minus: <sup>14</sup><sub>6</sub>C → <sup>14</sup><sub>7</sub>N + <sup>0</sup><sub>−1</sub>e + an electron antineutrino. Mass number stays 14; charge balances because 6 = 7 − 1. The electron is created in the decay, not removed from an orbital.</p><p>Optional quantitative energy extension: a rest-mass difference of 0.0200 u corresponds to E ≈ 0.0200 × 931.5 = 18.6 MeV. Explain conservation of total mass-energy while product rest masses can be lower.</p></div>

<div class="example"><h3>Honors extension • Clock error becomes distance error</h3><p>A timing error of 0.10 μs equals 1.0 × 10<sup>−7</sup> s. The corresponding light-travel range error is cΔt ≈ 30 m. Keeping this simple range error below 10 m requires |Δt| &lt; 3.34 × 10<sup>−8</sup> s = 0.0334 μs.</p><p>Model this conversion before combining signed gravitational and motion-related clock corrections. Explain that full navigation position error also depends on satellite geometry and estimation.</p></div>

